*======================================================================
* hello.a99 - the smallest SAMS Kernel application: one module that
* prints a line, echoes what is typed, and quits on FCTN =.
*======================================================================
* HOW TO READ THIS FILE.  This is the smallest complete program for the SAMS
* Kernel, and the best place to start: every other example is this plus more.
* Read it from the top; each line is explained.  The kernel (kernel/*.a99) is
* the platform: it starts the machine, checks the F18A video card and the SAMS
* memory card, sets up the screen and the keyboard, and then calls the three
* entry points of your MODULE once the module's code has been placed at >C000.
* A module is just assembly code that starts with a small table of entry
* addresses.
*
* What a program is on this system: INIT runs once when the module is switched
* to; TICK runs once for every video frame (60 times a second) with the key
* just typed in R0, and is where a program does its work; REDRAW is called
* when the whole screen must be drawn again (after a video mode change, or
* when you return to this module from another).  A module never has a main
* loop of its own: it does a little work in TICK and returns, which is what
* lets the kernel poll the keyboard, count frames and play music between
* calls.
*
* Two ways to build and run it (the guide's part 0 compares them): [PC]
* python3 tools/skbuild.py examples/hello makes a cartridge image; [TI] type
* or copy this file onto a disk, load the workbench cartridge, and use N, A
* and R.  The source is the same either way.
* Every routine name and equate the kernel exports (CLS, PUTS, KQUIT, COLS,
* APPRAM, QUITF ...) is defined by this file.  The build writes it
* (build/hello/kernel.equ); on the TI it is the file KERNEL/EQU on the disk.
* COPY pastes another file's text in at this point, as if you had typed it
* here.
* the kernel's symbols (routines, variables, equates)
       COPY  "kernel.equ"
       COPY "macros.a99"      .enter, .leave, .push, .pop, .callmod

* Colours.  The F18A's text mode gives every character cell a second byte, its
* ATTRIBUTE.  The attribute does not hold colours: it holds two ROLE numbers,
* a foreground role in the high four bits and a background role in the low
* four, and the current COLOUR SCHEME says what colour each role is.  RTEXT,
* RBG, RBARFG and RBARBG are role numbers from kdefs.a99.  Using roles means
* the user can change the whole colour scheme (the kernel does it on a key)
* without your program redrawing anything.
*
* The expression builds a byte and shifts it left 8 bits (*256) because the
* kernel's routines take the attribute in the HIGH byte of a register: the
* TMS9900's byte instructions (MOVB, CB ...) always work on the high byte of a
* register, and so most arguments to the kernel's routines are 'a byte in the
* high half'.  Note that xas99, and the assembler built into the workbench,
* evaluate an expression strictly left to right, so the parentheses matter.
ATEXT  EQU  (RTEXT*16+RBG)*256        text on the page background
ABAR   EQU  (RBARFG*16+RBARBG)*256    the status bar's colours

* Variables.  APPRAM is the kernel's name for the 7,168 bytes of ordinary RAM
* (>2300 to >3EFF) that belong to your application and are shared by all of
* its modules; you give names to offsets in it with EQU.  CURPOS holds the
* screen position (row * 80 + column) where the next typed character will go.
* EQU only gives a name to a number: it reserves nothing, so choosing the
* offsets is your job.
CURPOS EQU  APPRAM+0                   where the next typed character goes

* AORG places the code at the absolute address >C000, the start of the window
* ('slot C') at which the kernel maps every module's SAMS pages.  A module is
* always assembled for >C000.
       AORG >C000
* The module's entry table is the first thing at >C000, four words: the
* addresses of INIT, TICK and REDRAW and a reserved zero.  The kernel finds
* the module's routines through this table, so the order is fixed.  You may
* add your own entries after the zero for other modules to call (see
* examples/browser).
* the entry table: INIT, TICK, REDRAW, reserved
       DATA  INIT,TICK,REDRAW,0

* A routine in this system is called with BL (branch and link), which puts the
* return address in R11, and returns with RT (which is B *R11).  A routine
* that itself calls another routine with BL would lose its own return address
* in R11, so it saves it first with .enter and restores it with .leave: these
* are macros (macros.a99) that push and pop R11 on a small software stack kept
* in R10.  A routine that calls nothing (a 'leaf') needs neither and just ends
* with RT.
* INIT - the module has been switched to: draw the screen.
INIT   .enter
       BL   @REDRAW
       .leave
       RT

* CLS, FILLA, PUTS, PUTC are the kernel's text routines.  Arguments go in
* registers, results come back in registers, and each routine's header comment
* (kernel/text.a99, docs/ROUTINES.md) lists exactly which registers it changes
* ('Uses R0-R7'): that list matters, because a register you wanted to keep may
* be gone after the call.  Here every call is followed by loading fresh
* values, so nothing needs saving.
* REDRAW - the whole screen.
REDRAW .enter
       LI   R1,ATEXT
       BL   @CLS                       every cell a space in the text colours
       CLR  R0                         the top row as a bar
       LI   R1,ABAR
       LI   R2,COLS
       BL   @FILLA
       LI   R0,1
       LI   R1,TITLE
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTS
* A screen position is a single number: row * COLS + column, counting from 0
* at the top left.  COLS is the width of the current video mode (80 in the
* default Text 80 mode, 40 in Text 40), so writing 2*COLS+2 means 'row 2,
* column 2' whatever the mode.  Never write 80 here: the program would be
* wrong the moment the mode changes.
       LI   R0,2*COLS+2
       LI   R1,HELLO
       LI   R2,ATEXT
       BL   @PUTS
       LI   R0,4*COLS+2
       MOV  R0,@CURPOS
       .leave
       RT

* TICK - once a frame; R0 = the key typed (high byte) or 0.
* TICK is called once per frame.  R0 holds the key the user just pressed, as a
* code in the HIGH byte (so a key whose code is >41, the letter A, arrives as
* >4100), or 0 if no key was pressed this frame.  The kernel has already
* scanned the keyboard, turned FCTN and CTRL chords into codes, and applied
* key repeat.  MOV R0,R0 is the usual way to test a register against zero: any
* move sets the status flags, so the following JEQ (jump if equal) is taken
* when R0 was zero.
TICK   .enter
       MOV  R0,R0
       JEQ  TK9                        no key
       CI   R0,KQUIT*256               FCTN = ?
       JNE  TK1
       SETO @QUITF                     ask the kernel to quit
       JMP  TK9
* KQUIT is the code of FCTN = (the TI's QUIT key).  A program that wants to
* leave sets the variable QUITF to any non-zero value (SETO writes >FFFF) and
* the kernel ends the program cleanly at the end of this frame, switching the
* memory mapping off and returning to the console.  Below, a character is
* echoed only if its code is from >2000 to >7EFF, the printable ASCII range;
* CI compares R0 with an immediate value, and JL / JHE are unsigned jumps
* ('lower' and 'higher or equal'), the right ones for comparing character
* codes and addresses.
TK1    CI   R0,>2000                   a printable character (space to ~)?
       JL   TK9
       CI   R0,>7F00
       JHE  TK9
       MOV  R0,R1                      echo it
       MOV  @CURPOS,R0
       LI   R2,ATEXT
       BL   @PUTC
       INC  @CURPOS
TK9    .leave
       RT

* Strings are stored length first: STRI 'abc' assembles to the byte 3 followed
* by the three characters, which is the form PUTS expects.  EVEN pads to a
* word boundary: the TMS9900 reads words from even addresses only, so any
* instruction or word-sized data that follows byte data must be aligned.
TITLE  STRI 'Guest module running'
HELLO  STRI 'A module program on disk; FCTN = quits.'
       EVEN
